'''traducingly''' (trạ̄-dū′sing-li), ''adv.'' In a traducing or defamatory manner; slanderously; by way of defamation. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''traduct†''' (trạ̄-dukt′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''traductus'', pp. of ''traducere'', lead along, derive: see ''traduce.''] To derive or deduce; also, to transmit; propagate.

{{smaller block|No soul of man from seed ''traducted'' is.
{{right|''Dr. H. More'', Præ-existency of the Soul, st. 91.}}}}

'''traduct†''' (trạ̄-dukt′), ''n.'' [< L. ''traductus'', pp. of ''traducere'', transfer: see ''traduce.''] That which is transferred or translated; a translation.

{{smaller block|The ''Traduct'' may exceed the Original.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, ii. 47.}}}}

'''traduction''' (trạ̄-duk′shọn), ''n.'' [< F. ''traduction'' = Pr. ''traductio'' = Sp. ''traduccion'' = Pg. ''traducção'' = It. ''traduzione'', translation, < L. ''traductio(n-)'', < ''traducere'', pp. ''traductus'', lead across, transfer, propagate: see ''traduce.''] 1†. Derivation from one of the same kind; propagation; reproduction; transmission; inheritance.

{{smaller block|If by ''traduction'' came thy mind,
Our wonder is the less to find
A soul so charming from a stock so good;
Thy father was transfus'd into thy blood.
{{right|''Dryden'', To Mrs. Anne Killigrew, l. 23.}}}}

2†. Tradition; transmission from one to another.

{{smaller block|Traditional communication and ''traduction'' of truths.
{{right|''Sir M. Hale.''}}}}

3. The act of giving origin to a soul by procreation. Compare ''traducianism.''

{{smaller block|A third sort would have the soul of man (as of other liv-}}

