{{smaller block|From Billingsgate her fishy ''traffic'' bear.
{{right|''Gay'', Trivia, ii. 10.}}}}

'''Through traffic.''' {{smaller|See ''through''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''traffic''' (traf′ik), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''trafficked'', ppr. ''trafficking''. [Early mod. E. ''traffick'', ''traffike'', ''traf-''

readings.c3s1:
fique; < F. ''trafiquer'' = Sp. ''traficar'', ''trafagar'' = Pg. ''traficar'', ''trafeguear'' = It. ''trafficare'' (ML. refl. ''traficare'', ''traffigare''), traffic; from the noun.] I. ''intrans.'' 1. To trade; pass goods and commodities from one person to another for an equivalent in goods or money; buy and sell wares or commodities; carry on commerce.

{{smaller block|Despair to gain doth ''traffic'' oft for gaining.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 131.}}}}

{{smaller block|At twentie yeares they may ''traffike'', buy, sell, and circumuent all they can.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 193.}}}}

2. To deal; have business or dealings.

{{smaller block|It is a greate trauell to ''traffike'' or deale with furious, impatient, and men of euill suffering, for that they are importable to serue, and of conuersation verie perillous.
{{right|''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577), p. 116.}}}}

{{smaller block|How did you dare
To trade and ''traffic'' with Macbeth
In riddles and affairs of death?
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iii. 5. 4.}}}}

II. ''trans.'' 1. To exchange in traffic; barter, or buy and sell.

{{smaller block|In affairs
Of princes, subjects cannot ''traffic'' rights
Inherent to the crown.
{{right|''Ford'', Perkin Warbeck, iv. 1.}}}}

2. To bargain; negotiate; arrange. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|He ''trafficked'' the return of King James.
{{right|''Drummond'', Hist. James I., p. 14. (''Latham.'')}}}}

'''trafficable†''' (traf′ik-a-bl), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. ''traffiqueable''; < ''traffic(k)'' + ''-able''.] Capable of being disposed of in traffic; marketable.

{{smaller block|Money itself is not onely the price of all commodities in all civil nations, but it is also, in some cases, a ''traffiqueable'' commodity.
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Cases of Conscience, i. 1.}}}}
